%I A400125 #10 Oct 18 2026 16:00:21
%S A400125 0,0,0,0,1,0,1,1,0,1,2,0,1,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,1,3,1,2,3,2,2,2,1,1,1,1,3,
%T A400125 3,0,1,2,2,2,2,1,3,4,0,1,3,2,3,3,2,1,2,4,5,1,0,1,4,0,1,4,5,4,3,1,1,3,
%U A400125 1,3,3,0,1,3,3,1,1,1,4,3,0,1,5,1,2,2,1,3
%N A400125 a(n) is the number of nontrivial adjacent divisibilities in the strictly increasing half of row n of the Narayana triangle.
%C A400125 Let N(n, k) = binomial(n, k)*binomial(n, k - 1)/n be the Narayana number, so that N(n, k), 1 <= k <= n, form row n of triangle A001263. If T(m) = m*(m + 1)/2 is the m-th triangular number (A000217), then N(n, k + 1)/N(n, k) = T(n - k)/T(k). Hence a(n) = #{k : 2 <= k < n/2 and T(k) divides T(n - k)}. The restrictions exclude N(n, 1) = 1 | N(n, 2) and, for even n, the equal central pair.
%C A400125 Equivalently, N(n, k) divides N(n, k + 1) iff k divides n*(n + 1) and k + 1 divides (n + 1)*(n + 2).
%C A400125 For fixed k, this condition is periodic modulo k*(k + 1) and holds in exactly 2^(omega(k) + omega(k + 1)) residue classes, where omega = A001221. Hence its natural density is 2^(omega(k) + omega(k + 1))/(k*(k + 1)), which tends to 0 as k tends to infinity.
%C A400125 Let L_m = lcm(1, 2, ..., m). If m >= 3 and L_m divides n + 1, then N(n, 1) | N(n, 2) | ... | N(n, m), and hence a(n) >= m - 2. In particular, a(n) is unbounded.
%C A400125 The positive terms of A076708 are exactly the row indices n for which N(n, k + 1) = 2*N(n, k) for some k < n/2.
%H A400125 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400125/b400125.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>.
%H A400125 Miklós Bóna and Bruce E. Sagan, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL8/Sagan/sagan101.html">On Divisibility of Narayana Numbers by Primes</a>, Journal of Integer Sequences 8 (2005), Article 05.2.4.
%H A400125 Katalin Gyarmati, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5486/PMD.2009.4052">On the density of integers with consecutive divisors</a>, Publicationes Mathematicae Debrecen 74 (2009), 1-17.
%H A400125 Felix Huber, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23062219">Adjacent Divisibility in the Narayana Triangle</a>, Sep 30 2026.
%H A400125 Vladimir Pletser, <a href="https://doi.org/10.30538/oms2026.0299">Using Pell equation solutions to find all triangular numbers multiple of other triangular numbers</a>, Open Journal of Mathematical Sciences 10 (2026), 462-476.
%H A400125 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/NarayanaNumber.html">Narayana Number</a>.
%H A400125 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/TriangularNumber.html">Triangular Number</a>.
%F A400125 a(n) = #{k : 2 <= k < n/2, k divides n*(n + 1), and k + 1 divides (n + 1)*(n + 2)}.
%F A400125 sum_{n <= x} a(n) = C*x + O(x^(2/3)*(log x)^2), where C = sum_{k >= 2} 2^(omega(k) + omega(k + 1))/(k*(k + 1)).
%F A400125 lim_{x -> infinity} (1/x)*sum_{n <= x} a(n) = C.
%F A400125 If R(x) = max_{n <= x} a(n), then liminf_{x -> infinity} R(x)/log(x) >= 1.
%e A400125 a(14) = 3. The relevant indices are k = 2, 3, 5, since N(14, 2) = 91 divides N(14, 3) = 2366, N(14, 3) = 2366 divides N(14, 4) = 26026, and N(14, 5) = 143143 divides N(14, 6) = 429429. This is the first n for which a(n) = 3.
%p A400125 A400125 := proc(n)
%p A400125     option remember;
%p A400125     local a, k;
%p A400125     a := 0;
%p A400125     for k from 2 while 2*k < n do
%p A400125         if irem(n*(n + 1), k) = 0 and irem((n + 1)*(n + 2), k + 1) = 0 then
%p A400125             a := a + 1;
%p A400125         end if;
%p A400125     end do;
%p A400125     a;
%p A400125 end proc:
%p A400125 seq(A400125(n), n = 1 .. 88);
%Y A400125 Cf. A000217, A001221, A001263, A076708.
%K A400125 nonn,new
%O A400125 1,11
%A A400125 _Felix Huber_, Oct 11 2026
